    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment of the Offer\n\n**It is a strong initial offer, but it has clear weaknesses you can exploit.**\n\n#### The Math:\n* **Current Meta (IC6):** ~$526,000/yr ($240k base + $36k target bonus + $250k equity run-rate).\n* **Google (L6) Offer:**\n  * **Base Salary:** $235,000 (a slight step down from your $240k Meta base).\n  * **Target Bonus:** $47,000 (20% target at Google L6 vs. 15% at Meta IC6).\n  * **Equity (GSUs):** $1,080,000 over 4 years.\n  * **Sign-on Bonus:** $0.\n\n#### The Google Equity Nuance (Front-Loaded Vesting):\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)** for new-hire equity:\n* **Year 1:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 2:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 3:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $237.6k equity = **~$519,600** (before refreshers)\n* **Year 4:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $129.6k equity = **~$411,600** (before refreshers)\n\n**Verdict:** In Years 1 and 2, this is a **~21% increase** over your current Meta compensation. However, because you are taking a base salary cut, receiving $0 sign-on bonus, and leaving stable unvested equity at a company you like, **you should not accept this first offer as-is.**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: Negotiate (Do Not Accept or Walk Away Yet)\n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n1. **You are a passive candidate:** Google reached out to you; you are happy at Meta and have no urgency to leave.\n2. **Base pay deficit:** Google\u2019s initial base is lower than your current salary ($235k vs. $240k), despite their top of band being $271k.\n3. **No sign-on bonus:** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses at L6 to close strong candidates.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For & Exact Targets\n\n| Component | Initial Offer | What to Counter With | Realistic Landing Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **4-Year Equity** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000** | $1,120,000 \u2013 $1,150,000 |\n\n#### Why these specific numbers?\n* **Base Salary ($255k ask):** The range is $183k\u2013$271k. Stating that you cannot take a step backward from your current $240k base gives the recruiter an indisputable business justification for compensation committee approval.\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($75k ask):** Sign-on bonuses come out of a separate one-time budget and are the easiest lever for a Google recruiter to pull without hitting band ceilings. This bridges any lost near-term Meta vesting/bonus cycle.\n* **Equity ($1.2M ask):** $1.08M is solid for L6 PM, but pushing to $1.2M (~$300k/yr flat equivalent) brings top-of-band parity with Meta IC6.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation (Script)\n\nYou can deliver this over the phone or in a follow-up email:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role. That said, as I mentioned from our first conversation, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and I\u2019m in a very strong spot at Meta.*\n>\n> *Looking at the numbers, the total opportunity is compelling in the first two years, but there are a few gaps that make it difficult to justify a move right now:*\n> 1. *The base salary ($235k) is below my current base ($240k).*\n> 2. *There is no sign-on bonus to offset the unvested equity and performance bonus timing I\u2019d be walking away from in the immediate term.*\n>\n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000***\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000***\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,200,000** over 4 years*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap to those numbers, I would be thrilled to sign and give my notice.\"*",
